# Triangle - one cycle: trace a triangle between three poses.
#
# Three joint poses [rad]; the arm movej's between them, so base (joint 0),
# shoulder (joint 1) and elbow (joint 2) all move. A movej between two poses is a
# straight line in joint space, so its geometry is fixed and vel/acc only set the
# speed along it. Analyze a joint with --joint.
#
# Edit vel and acc below, then run it with send.py (--loop N repeats the cycle).
# Plain assignments (no `global`): send.py wraps this in a repeat loop, and
# URScript does not allow a `global` declaration inside a loop.
#
# The values below are NOT the PPO agent's output: they come from a direct grid
# search over the same distilled model (models/distill_ur5.pkl) and the same
# objective the agent optimises (train_rla.OBJECTIVE, SCORE_WEIGHT=5000).
# Against the baseline in scripts/triangle.script the model predicts
#
#     score  0.008070 -> 0.005496  (-31.9%)
#     cycle  0.929s   -> 1.029s    (+10.8%)
#
# i.e. ~32% less tracking error for ~11% more cycle time. Both trained agents
# (models/agent_test100_params.zip, models/agent_tri.zip) scored worse than the
# baseline here: the reward surface has a narrow optimum next to the grid's
# worst point, which a Gaussian policy over a one-step bandit explores poorly.
#
# Note: keep "<name> = <number>" out of these comments. utils.get_param takes the
# first such match in the file, so a commented example would be read as the
# script's actual vel/acc.

vel = 60
acc = 200

# Three corners of the triangle [rad]; the cycle is C1 -> C2 -> C3 -> C1.
CORNER_1 = [ 0.0, -1.4, 1.4, -1.57, -1.57, 0.0]
CORNER_2 = [ 0.5, -1.1, 1.1, -1.57, -1.57, 0.0]
CORNER_3 = [-0.5, -1.1, 1.1, -1.57, -1.57, 0.0]

write_output_float_register(1, vel)
write_output_float_register(2, acc)
movej(CORNER_1, a=acc, v=vel)
sleep(0.8)
movej(CORNER_2, a=acc, v=vel)
sleep(0.8)
movej(CORNER_3, a=acc, v=vel)
sleep(0.8)
movej(CORNER_1, a=acc, v=vel)
sleep(0.8)
